#!/bin/bash
# land-gallery-wall.sh — the Beauty hub gets the owner's backdrop.
#
#   1. THE GROUND GOES NEAR-BLACK, grained and vignetted. The prints stay cream:
#      every plate, card, field and photograph is paper, and the wall is only
#      ever behind them. First grant in the city to re-point TWO surfaces.
#   2. THE BUDGET QUESTION LEAVES THE DETAILS FORM. It was asked twice — six
#      chips here, the real slider in its own plate — and the chips were the
#      half blocking the form from ever counting itself finished.
#   3. ONE PRODUCT, ONE PLACE TO BUY IT. A cleanser used morning and evening is
#      one bottle; the second appearance now points at the first instead of
#      offering a second "Add to bag".
#   4. THREE SURFACES, NO FOURTH — plate, leaf, sheet. The progress strip, the
#      history and the biomarker panel stop being rounded emoji cards.
#   5. THE BAG BAR STOPS FLOATING and becomes the last block of the page.
#   6. THE ROUTINE STEP BECOMES A CARD — big photograph on top, everything
#      underneath, from the owner's catalogue sheet. Same layout, same facts.
#
# WEB APP ONLY (together-city-react). Vercel. No API change, no migration.
# PRECONDITION: land-sand-and-plates.sh.
set -euo pipefail

cd "$(dirname "$0")"
[ -d together-city-chat ] || { echo "!! Run this from the Together-Ai repo root."; exit 1; }

if [ -f .git/index.lock ] && [ ! -s .git/index.lock ]; then
  echo "== clearing a stale empty .git/index.lock"
  rm -f .git/index.lock
fi

NEEDS="Prints on a dark wall"
LOG="$(git log --oneline -40)"
case "$LOG" in
  *"$NEEDS"*) echo "== \"$NEEDS\" is already here. Nothing to do."; exit 0 ;;
esac
case "$LOG" in
  *"A room the colour of what it sells"*) ;;
  *) echo "!! Run land-sand-and-plates.sh first — this is written against the tree it produces."; exit 1 ;;
esac

ALLOWED='^ M together-city-react/(public/assets/img/(apple-touch-icon-180|tc-icon-1024|tc-icon-192|tc-icon-512|tc-icon-maskable-512)\.png|public/downloads/TogetherCity\.apk)$'
DIRTY="$(git status --porcelain \
  | grep -Ev '^\?\? (land-.*\.sh|push-.*\.sh|.*\.patch)$' \
  | grep -Ev "$ALLOWED" || true)"
if [ -n "$DIRTY" ]; then
  echo "!! The tree carries changes this script did not expect:"; echo "$DIRTY"; exit 1
fi

PATCH="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/land.XXXXXX")"
trap 'rm -f "$PATCH"' EXIT
cat <<'B64EOF' | tr -d '\n' | openssl base64 -d -A > "$PATCH"

B64EOF
WANT="45a6bce979b5595e4b5421aac32c17caa8326c101816fe442fcee49e29899aea"
GOT="$(shasum -a 256 "$PATCH" | awk '{print $1}')"
[ "$GOT" = "$WANT" ] || { echo "!! Patch is corrupt."; echo "   want $WANT"; echo "   got  $GOT"; exit 1; }
echo "== patch verified"
git apply --check -C1 "$PATCH" || { echo "!! Patch does not apply cleanly. Nothing written."; exit 1; }
git apply -C1 "$PATCH"
echo "== applied"

echo "== gates: the web app"
cd together-city-react
npx tsc --noEmit
npx vitest run
node scripts/a11y-audit.mjs
node scripts/motion-ceiling.mjs
node scripts/lint-ceiling.mjs
npx vite build
cd ..

git add -A together-city-react/src
git commit -F - <<'MSG'
Prints on a dark wall

── THE GROUND GOES NEAR-BLACK, AND THE PAPER DOES NOT ─────────────────────────

The owner's backdrop photograph, as the Beauty hub's ground: near-black, grained,
lit from up and to the left. Every plate, card, field and photograph stays cream.
The wall is only ever behind things, and the edge between the two is what makes
a plate read as a plate.

THIS IS THE FIRST GRANT IN THE CITY TO RE-POINT TWO SURFACES. Every one before it
moved a single ground — the page and the card were the same material a step
apart, so --ink was the answer to "what colour is text" everywhere in the hub.
Here they are different materials, and that costs three things, all of them
written down rather than discovered later.

FIRST, A SECOND INK SCALE. --on-ground / -soft / -muted are declared at :root as
the ordinary scale, so no hub without a wall is affected, and re-pointed here to
near-whites measured on the wall. relief.spec now checks them against --ground
the way it checks --ink against --paper: a hub with two grounds and one checked
ground is a hub with an unchecked ground, and it is the one nobody screenshots —
a heading, a tab rule, an index between two plates.

SECOND, THE WALL'S INK IS APPLIED BY INHERITANCE, NOT BY CLASS. Re-pointing --ink
inside .tc-main reaches every descendant including the several thousand inline
`color: 'var(--ink)'` styles in this codebase, which no class could ever have
reached; every lit surface then puts the paper's near-black back, once, in one
list. The alternative was auditing four pages by eye for anything not inside a
card — a job that is never finished, because the next component somebody adds to
a beauty page is dark ink on a black wall and looks fine in review.

Putting it back needs the paper's value under a name .tc-main did not shadow, so
the four greys are declared as --on-paper* and --ink now points at them. Which
exposed a real hole: relief.spec's AA check read hexes only and skipped anything
else, so `--ink: var(--on-paper)` would have un-checked a hub's whole ink scale
and the suite would have gone green. It follows one hop now, and both new guards
were confirmed to fail on a bad value before this was committed.

THIRD, ONE FIXED LAYER INSTEAD OF TWO. Social's sky was hung by a `:has()`-scoped
::before; a background painted per element tiles its vignette once per element
and the seams show the moment anything scrolls. The wall is the same object, so
the rule is now unscoped rather than copied — it is safe everywhere because
--sky-image is `none` at :root and --ground is already what body paints.

The texture is generated, not shipped: a 2000px photograph of a black wall is a
megabyte and a half for something four lines of CSS say exactly, and it would
need re-exporting for every screen density.

The ground went down in two steps and the first is worth keeping. It was warm
sand for an afternoon, on exactly this argument — skincare is photographed
cut-out, near-white on near-white, and casing cannot fix a subject that is the
colour of the page. Sand answers that. Near-black answers it completely rather
than adequately, which is the honest reason the value moved.

── THE BUDGET LEAVES THE DETAILS FORM ─────────────────────────────────────────

It was asked twice: six range chips at the foot of the profile form, and the real
budget — a slider, a live plan, the per-category split — in its own plate on the
other tab. Two questions, one answer.

Removing the chips means removing the REQUIREMENT, and that is the whole fix.
`budget` was one of the eighteen fields the completion count reads; delete the
field and leave the gate and the form can never finish, never collapse, and the
analysis stays locked behind a question that is no longer on the page. That is
the trap in deleting a field — the field goes and the gate it fed stays. The
count is seventeen now, and the saved-profile summary no longer prints a budget
row that a slider has since moved past.

── ONE PRODUCT, ONE PLACE TO BUY IT ───────────────────────────────────────────

A cleanser used morning and evening is one bottle, and it appeared as two steps
each with its own quantity control. The bag was never wrong — both edited the
same line, keyed by productId — but the page said otherwise twice over: two "Add
to bag" buttons for one purchase, and after adding, two steppers reading "1" that
a reasonable person reads as two bottles.

The second appearance now says where the first one is. It keeps its number in the
order, its own instruction and its own frequency, because "massage into damp
skin" at night is still a thing you do at night. And it names the BAND rather
than saying "already in your bag", because what somebody wants to know when a
product turns up twice is whether they have missed a second one to buy, and the
answer to that is a place.

── AND THREE SURFACES, WITH NO FOURTH ─────────────────────────────────────────

A plate for the four chapters the owner drew, a leaf for the contents of one,
and a SHEET for everything that is simply content — the progress strip, the
dated history, the biomarker panel, the bag's running total. Same cream paper,
same cut edge, same lift off the wall, none of the masthead.

Those four were the last objects still wearing the city's ordinary idiom:
rounded white tiles headed with an emoji, in the middle of a set of prints. The
emoji went with the headings; the one that stays is the 🩸 inside a condition
chip, which means "your labs support this one" and is doing work no word does in
that width.

── AND THE BAG COMES OFF THE VIEWPORT ─────────────────────────────────────────

It was `position: sticky` with a heavy drop shadow, so on the routine sheet it
rode up the screen covering the very steps somebody was reading — a summary of
what is in the bag parked across the products they were deciding whether to put
in it. It is the last block of the page now. You reach it by getting to the end,
which is also when you have finished deciding.

── AND THE ROUTINE STEP BECOMES A CARD ────────────────────────────────────────

The owner's catalogue sheet, applied to a step: a tall well with the product
photographed in it, and everything else written underneath.

WHY THE PICTURE GOT BIGGER. It was 62 square in a row beside four lines of text
— enough to tell a bottle from a tube and not enough to tell one white bottle
from another. On a page whose whole job is "go and buy these eight things", the
photograph IS the identifier: it is what somebody matches against a shelf or a
search result. The text is what they read once.

THE LAYOUT DID NOT CHANGE, and that was the instruction. Morning and evening
still sit side by side, the steps are still in order, and every fact the row
carried is still on the card — the step, the tier, the price, the monthly cost,
the pack size, how long it lasts, the instruction, the frequency, the warnings,
the quantity control. Only the shape moved. A band is a small grid inside its
own column, so it is two cards abreast at desk width and one on a phone.

The order badge went onto the picture rather than being dropped. A grid of
product cards loses the one thing a numbered list had, and a routine whose steps
are not visibly in order is a catalogue.

Backend untouched: no route, no schema, no query changed.
MSG

echo "== committed"
cat <<'DONE'

===============================================================
 Landed: Prints on a dark wall
 Push — Vercel rebuilds. /beauty/profile is cream plates on a
 black backdrop; the details form has no budget chips and
 collapses to its summary once saved.
===============================================================

DONE
